{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n108 \n \n \nN.5 Outreach \nNASA \nProject \nScientist \nDennis \nStocker \ncreated \nthe \nSpace \nFlames \nFacebook \npage \n(www.facebook.com/space.flames) and Flickr site (https://www.flickr.com/photos/space-flames), \nwhere some entries are specific to Flame Design.  He continues to administer these sites, where most \ncontent thus far has been about the ACME project of which Flame Design is a part.  As an example of \neducational outreach, a simple board game related to the ACME\u2019s spherical flames experiments can \nbe downloaded from https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/space/iss-research/iss-fcf/cir/acme/downloads/.  \nACME fact sheets, papers, posters, and presentations providing a project overview and status can also \nbe downloaded from that page.  Printed copies of fact sheets have been distributed at meetings of \nThe Combustion Institute and the American Society for Gravitational and Space Research (ASGSR). \n \nThe investigators, Washington University in St. Louis, and the University of Maryland promoted \nawareness of the Flame Design investigation, as can be seen by these examples: \n\u2022 \nFlame Design \n\u2022 \nFires in Microgravity \n\u2022 \n\u2018Playing\u2019 with fire (30 Sept. 2004) \n\u2022 \nUMD Experiments Launched into Orbit via SpaceX Capsule (6 June 2017) \n\u2022 \nExperiment designed by Washington University engineer launched on SpaceX (8 June 2017) \n\u2022 \nFlame design in space may lead to soot-free fire (9 May 2019) \u2013 one of 2019\u2019s top-ten stories \nfrom the Washington University in St. Louis\u2019 McKelvey School of Engineering \n\u2022 \nIn the media: International Space Station experiments will search for key to soot-free flames \n(15 May 2019) \n\u2022 \nEngineering Momentum Magazine: Spring 2020 (cover story) \n\u2022 \nUnlocking the Mysteries of Fire ... in Space (10 Feb. 2021)  \n\u2022 \nFire Protection Experts Available to the Media (22 June 2021) \n\u2022 \nSunderland Gives Invited Talk at ASGSR 2021 Symposium (5 Nov. 2021) \n \nNASA articles, etc. about the study (and ISS combustion research in general) can be seen in these \nexamples: \n\u2022 \nSpace Life and Physical Sciences Research and Applications SpaceX CRS-11 Payloads and \nExperiments (1 June 2017) \n\u2022 \nThe Power of Studying Combustion on the ISS (13 March 2018) \n\u2022 \nStudying flames in microgravity is helping make combustion on Earth cleaner, and space safer, \nwhere the video can be downloaded from Designing Flames Aboard ISS \n\u2022 \nAstronaut Christina Koch\u2019s Space Station Science Scrapbook (Flame Design set up seen at", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 108}}